{-
   `@heap` on a plain binding, across the three ways one can be written - the flat form
   (`let @heap &x = e`), the scoped statement form (`let @heap x = e in:`) and the scoped value form
   (`let @heap x = e in x + ...`).

   All three name exactly one object, so all three used to answer `@heap` differently. The flat form
   read the attribute after building its local, in `resolveDeclaration`. The scoped forms both go
   through `resolveScoped`, which never read it at all - and `resolveDeclaration`'s own `let ... in`
   branch called `readBindingAttributes` with `placed` hard-wired `false` for every `let ... in`
   whatever its shape, which is what made a *single-name* scoped `@heap` refused as "not one name"
   rather than honoured. `bindPlainLocal` is now the one place both `resolveDeclaration`'s flat
   branch and `resolveScoped`'s plain branch build a local from a resolved value - attribute read
   included - so a decision that used to have three call sites has one, and the three forms agree.

   Marked rather than shared: the shipping stage placed a *scalar* copy in a register and had
   nothing for `@heap` to be about there ("this binding names a value that occupies no storage of
   its own"), where this stage's bindings are place-based from the start and always have a slot -
   `Reject.Heap.yana`'s own header makes the same point about a `TrivialCopy` binding.

   `gate` is a mutable global rather than a literal, so that none of the three folds away before the
   `.mir.expect` can show its local still marked `heap`.
-}

let &gate = 3

fn flatForm() -> Int:
    let @heap &p = gate
    return p

fn statementForm() -> Int:
    let @heap p = gate in:
        return p

fn valueForm() -> Int = let @heap p = gate in p + 1

fn main() -> Int = flatForm() + statementForm() + valueForm()
